This is the **memos** repo (`github.com/usememos/memos`). Third-party SSO is wired through a small Go IdP layer under `internal/idp/`.

## Provider abstraction

**Import path:** `github.com/usememos/memos/internal/idp`  
**Source file:** `/bench/repo/internal/idp/idp.go`

The abstraction is the shared user-info type that every protocol implementation must produce. There is no explicit Go `interface` type; the contract is implicit via `*idp.IdentityProviderUserInfo`.

```1:8:/bench/repo/internal/idp/idp.go
package idp

type IdentityProviderUserInfo struct {
	Identifier  string
	DisplayName string
	Email       string
	AvatarURL   string
}
```

The only other Go file in that package is under `oauth2/`; `internal/idp` itself defines this struct only.

---

## Concrete protocol implementation shipped

**Import path:** `github.com/usememos/memos/internal/idp/oauth2`  
**Source file:** `/bench/repo/internal/idp/oauth2/oauth2.go`

The shipped implementation is **OAuth 2.0** (authorization-code flow with optional PKCE, token exchange, and UserInfo fetch).

Package declaration and type:

```1:22:/bench/repo/internal/idp/oauth2/oauth2.go
